meta {
  name: Lend a copy
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{host}}/loans
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
    "userId": "{{userId}}",
    "loanDays": 14
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  // A fresh key per run, so this request genuinely lends rather than replaying the loan a
  // previous run made. The next two requests reuse whatever this one set, which is what lets
  // them demonstrate a replay and a conflict against a loan that exists right now.
  //
  // Pin it with `--env-var idempotencyKey=...` to retry a specific key by hand.
  if (!bru.getEnvVar("pinIdempotencyKey")) {
    bru.setVar("idempotencyKey", `bruno-loan-${Date.now()}`);
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body.data && res.body.data.id) {
    bru.setVar("loanId", res.body.data.id);
  }
}

docs {
  201. Stores the new loan's id as loanId.

  The Idempotency-Key header is required, not optional: without it this is a 400.

  Underneath, the copy is taken by a single conditional UPDATE whose WHERE clause carries the
  rule (is_active AND available_copies > 0), and the loan row, the audit entry and the
  idempotency record are committed with it or not at all.

  Two requests racing for the last copy produce exactly one 201 and one 409. To see it, seed a
  book with totalCopies 1, register a second reader, and send this twice at once.

  409 also covers: the book is withdrawn, and this reader already holds a copy of this title -
  the last enforced by a partial unique index over active loans, not only by a check the
  handler makes.
}
